    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Both tests pass and the full build succeeds. \n\n## Summary\n\nOwner deletion is now a soft-delete. Changes made:\n\n**Behavior**\n- `DeleteOwner.java` \u2014 instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`, now sets `owner.setDeleted(true)` and saves, so `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` returns 2xx and retains the row.\n- `RejectDuplicateIdentity.java` / `RejectDuplicateHousehold.java` \u2014 each loop now guards with `!owner.isDeleted()`, so the create endpoint's identity and household duplicate checks ignore soft-deleted owners (a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only match is deleted).\n\n**Model / persistence**\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added a `boolean deleted` field (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`. A primitive defaults to `false`, so newly created owners are not deleted.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a `deleted` boolean property to the `Owner` response schema; MapStruct auto-maps it, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` exposes `$.deleted` (no mapper edit needed).\n- `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` + `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 added the `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` column and the trailing value on the seed rows (this is the only maintained/in-sync schema; the h2/mysql/postgres stubs already lack most entity columns and aren't used by the app or tests).\n\n**Structural-impact notes**: I kept each touched function trivial (a one-token guard on two checks, a two-line body on `DeleteOwner`, plain accessors on `Owner`) rather than growing any existing method or the large `Owner` class's methods, and relied on the generated DTO + auto-mapping to avoid touching `OwnerMapper`.",
